# Example .wslconfig for the CEC compute-plane box (RTX 5090 / 192 GB / Core Ultra 7 265K).
# COPY this to  %UserProfile%\.wslconfig  on Windows (NOT into the repo), then run:  wsl --shutdown
# Docs: https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/wsl/wsl-config
#
# Rationale (see docs/local-compute-exploration.md):
#   - The Freerouting swarm wants most cores (one ~0.5 GB JVM per core); 18 leaves ~2 for Windows.
#   - 128 GB to WSL2 leaves ~64 GB for the Windows host; plenty for parallel JVMs + FEM meshes.
#   - GPU passthrough needs the WINDOWS NVIDIA driver only -- do NOT install a Linux driver in WSL2.
#   - Use WSL2 >= 2.7.0 (CUDA-graph capture fix for Blackwell): check with  wsl --version

[wsl2]
memory=128GB
processors=18
swap=0
localhostForwarding=true

# After editing: `wsl --shutdown`, then verify the GPU is visible inside the distro:
#   nvidia-smi
#   docker run --rm --gpus all nvidia/cuda:12.8.0-base-ubuntu24.04 nvidia-smi
# If the 5090 isn't seen in a container, run `nvidia-cdi-refresh` (the documented Blackwell-in-WSL fix).
